Cell["\<\
The curried setup converts each point to lattice coordinates using \
LinearSolve[Transpose[unitBasis], point]. generator acts on those coordinates \
and must return a matrix.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->1228773496,ExpressionUUID->"ef071b28-93f3-b748-8fed-e6dac7204232"],

Cell["\<\
The Hamiltonian is transformed to the sorted eigenbasis of chiralOperator. \
The implementation extracts a square off-diagonal block of dimension 2 \
Length[points], so the intended full Hamiltonian and chiralOperator \
dimensions are 4 Length[points].\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->1605726482,ExpressionUUID->"26471706-1727-9643-89c7-3e2e76db6803"],

Cell["\<\
A singular-value decomposition of this block supplies its unitary polar \
factor q. The coordinate matrix m is a block diagonal of MatrixExp[2 Pi I \
generator[coordinate]].\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->816035514,ExpressionUUID->"62c9f0cd-1101-014f-8352-cc47f7830257"],

Cell["\<\
Returns Tr[MatrixLog[m.q.ConjugateTranspose[m].ConjugateTranspose[q]]]/(2 Pi \
I). With a list of generators, returns one value per generator and uses \
ParallelTable to build the coordinate blocks.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->1657259423,ExpressionUUID->"98582182-0307-5449-bfb4-198614106581"],

Cell["\<\
The single-generator example uses a gapped trivial chiral model and runs \
serially. A physical invariant requires compatible chiral sectors, generator \
dimensions, boundary conditions, and a nonsingular relevant block.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->755974603,ExpressionUUID->"8b3b6535-b11a-bb44-9fcb-0a6d01023130"]
}, Closed]],

